        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(apple): share chat gateway payload mapping",
        "body": "Closes #106148\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe native iOS and macOS chat transports independently decoded the same gateway payloads, mapped the same events, normalized the same commands, and parsed the same session keys. The copies had already drifted around model display-name fallback behavior and made protocol changes require parallel edits.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nMove canonical gateway payload decoding and session-key parsing into the shared `OpenClawChatUI` package. Both platform transports now keep only platform-owned connection, routing, logging, and lifecycle behavior while calling the shared codec for protocol mapping. Shared unit coverage exercises wait responses, models, commands, events, and session identity.\n\nAI-assisted: Codex implemented and reviewed this refactor.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo intended change to normal chat behavior. Blank model display names now consistently fall back to the model ID on both native Apple apps.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `xcrun swiftc -parse` on all changed Swift sources and tests\n- strict SwiftLint on changed iOS, macOS, and shared Swift files: 0 violations\n- `git diff --check`\n- Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kxd6xbcesmststartbkjxyky`: focused protocol coverage tests (12/12) and apps/tooling `pnpm check:changed` passed\n- `node scripts/check-protocol-event-coverage.mjs`: 33 gateway events fully handled or allowlisted\n- mandatory autoreviews: no findings (0.90 implementation; 0.93 CI-guard fix)\n- hosted macOS Swift tests and iOS build required before merge\n",
